---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CurrencyRiskLab from "../../components/CurrencyRiskLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Currency Risk: Hedged vs. Unhedged"
  description="Own a foreign stock or bond and you own its currency too. See how currency swings add volatility — a lot for calm bonds, a little for volatile stocks — and why the rule of thumb is to hedge bonds but rarely stocks."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Should you hedge the currency?</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Buy an international fund and you're making two bets, not one: on the assets,
      and on their currency. Those exchange-rate swings add risk. Hedging strips
      them out — but it's worth the trouble for some assets far more than others.
      See exactly when it matters.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <CurrencyRiskLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>One ratio decides it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It's currency vol vs. asset vol.</strong> A currency that swings
          ~9% a year is enormous next to a bond that swings ~5%, and minor next to
          a stock that swings ~16%. Hedging removes that currency swing, so its
          value depends entirely on how big it is relative to the asset.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hedge your foreign bonds.</strong> Left unhedged, currency can be
          the dominant source of a bond fund's volatility — often roughly doubling
          it. Hedging brings it back down for essentially no cost to expected
          return, which is why it's the standard advice.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Don't sweat your foreign stocks.</strong> Currency adds only a
          little to already-volatile equities, and can even diversify them (some
          currencies rise when markets fall). Most global stock investors leave the
          currency unhedged, and it barely matters over the long run.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This is an illustrative model, not real exchange-rate data — the point is
        the mechanism, not a forecast. Currency hedging is close to free over long
        horizons, so the whole question is simply how much risk it removes.
        Educational only, not financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="currency-risk" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/currency-risk" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
